1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:01,393
You wanted land.

2
00:00:01,393 --> 00:00:02,309
Lots of it.

3
00:00:02,309 --> 00:00:03,811
Wide green meadows,

4
00:00:03,811 --> 00:00:06,261
a river full of fish, and not a single

5
00:00:06,261 --> 00:00:09,123
neighbor arguing about a boundary stone.

6
00:00:09,123 --> 00:00:11,271
Instead, it is barely light,

7
00:00:11,271 --> 00:00:13,394
you are being sick over the side of a

8
00:00:13,394 --> 00:00:15,822
cargo ship in the middle of the ocean,

9
00:00:15,822 --> 00:00:17,811
and a cow is standing on your foot.

10
00:00:18,122 --> 00:00:20,129
Somebody always believes the grass is

11
00:00:20,129 --> 00:00:22,786
greener on the other side of the sea.

12
00:00:22,786 --> 00:00:25,121
This year, that somebody is you.

13
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:26,732
Imagine you are Grim,

14
00:00:26,732 --> 00:00:29,508
Eirik's neighbor from our earlier chapters,

15
00:00:29,508 --> 00:00:31,644
the man with the boundary stone.

16
00:00:31,644 --> 00:00:33,744
Like Eirik and Aslaug,

17
00:00:33,744 --> 00:00:35,430
you are an imagined person,

18
00:00:35,430 --> 00:00:39,950
built from what archaeology, laws and written sources tell us,

19
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:41,937
not a real man from history.

20
00:00:41,937 --> 00:00:44,279
You have short dark-brown hair,

21
00:00:44,813 --> 00:00:48,329
a short neat dark beard, and an olive-green wool

22
00:00:48,329 --> 00:00:51,449
tunic that has seen a great many arguments.

23
00:00:51,449 --> 00:00:53,497
You are not a bad man.

24
00:00:53,497 --> 00:00:54,814
You simply believe,

25
00:00:54,814 --> 00:00:57,935
very sincerely, that boundary stones have a

26
00:00:57,935 --> 00:01:00,340
habit of wandering away from your land.

27
00:01:00,698 --> 00:01:02,464
You have heard the stories.

28
00:01:02,464 --> 00:01:05,497
Out in the ocean to the west lies Iceland,

29
00:01:05,497 --> 00:01:07,085
where a man can claim

30
00:01:07,085 --> 00:01:10,123
as much land as he can walk around.

31
00:01:10,123 --> 00:01:11,019
No fences.

32
00:01:11,019 --> 00:01:12,430
No stones.

33
00:01:12,430 --> 00:01:13,223
No Eirik.

34
00:01:13,223 --> 00:01:15,479
So you sell your farm,

35
00:01:15,479 --> 00:01:18,480
and load your wife Halla, your two grown sons,

36
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:21,177
your cattle, your sheep and your

37
00:01:21,177 --> 00:01:24,286
opinions onto a ship, and sail west.

38
00:01:24,286 --> 00:01:26,560
Eirik watches you go from the landing.

39
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:28,214
He does not wave.

40
00:01:28,214 --> 00:01:30,018
Later, he will admit that the

41
00:01:30,018 --> 00:01:32,415
valley feels strangely quiet.

42
00:01:32,415 --> 00:01:33,681
He will never admit that

43
00:01:33,681 --> 00:01:35,248
he misses the arguments.

44
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:39,935
Popular culture imagines Iceland as an empty frontier,

45
00:01:39,935 --> 00:01:42,280
waiting for brave pioneers.

46
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:43,907
It was, once.

47
00:01:43,907 --> 00:01:46,592
But you are setting out around the

48
00:01:46,592 --> 00:01:47,651
year nine hundred and fifty,

49
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:51,299
and in Iceland, that is very late indeed.

50
00:01:51,721 --> 00:01:53,437
And at the end of this story,

51
00:01:53,437 --> 00:01:56,184
we will meet a man who gave a land of ice and

52
00:01:56,184 --> 00:01:58,456
rock a very attractive name,

53
00:01:58,456 --> 00:02:01,338
for reasons any estate agent would understand.

54
00:02:01,984 --> 00:02:03,293
Your ship is a sturdy

55
00:02:03,293 --> 00:02:04,962
ocean-going cargo ship.

56
00:02:05,431 --> 00:02:07,512
Archaeologists raised one like it from

57
00:02:07,512 --> 00:02:08,943
a Danish fjord,

58
00:02:08,943 --> 00:02:12,709
built in western Norway some decades after your time:

59
00:02:12,709 --> 00:02:15,435
about sixteen metres long, with open holds in

60
00:02:15,435 --> 00:02:18,466
the middle and small decks at each end.

61
00:02:18,466 --> 00:02:20,085
The holds are for cargo.

62
00:02:20,085 --> 00:02:23,168
This year, the cargo is your entire life.

63
00:02:23,168 --> 00:02:24,912
Sacks of grain,

64
00:02:24,912 --> 00:02:29,655
tools, timber, cooking pots, a loom, and as many animals as the

65
00:02:29,655 --> 00:02:32,261
ship can carry without sinking or mutiny.

66
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:34,892
The historian Jesse Byock lists what

67
00:02:34,892 --> 00:02:36,998
the settlers brought with them:

68
00:02:37,517 --> 00:02:43,328
dogs, cats, pigs, goats, sheep, cattle and horses.

69
00:02:43,328 --> 00:02:45,684
Bones from the earliest farms

70
00:02:45,684 --> 00:02:48,052
in Iceland tell the same story.

71
00:02:48,052 --> 00:02:49,923
A whole farmyard crossed

72
00:02:49,923 --> 00:02:51,710
the ocean in open boats.

73
00:02:51,710 --> 00:02:54,295
There is no shelter from the spray.

74
00:02:54,295 --> 00:02:55,856
The cows are frightened,

75
00:02:55,856 --> 00:02:59,350
the sheep are seasick, and your sons are arguing about

76
00:02:59,350 --> 00:03:01,998
whose turn it is to clean up after both.

77
00:03:01,998 --> 00:03:03,556
Halla has stopped speaking to

78
00:03:03,556 --> 00:03:05,788
you somewhere around the second day.

79
00:03:05,788 --> 00:03:09,668
Then, one morning, Iceland rises out of the grey:

80
00:03:09,668 --> 00:03:10,461
black sand,

81
00:03:10,461 --> 00:03:14,500
dark cliffs, mountains with snow on them in the middle of summer,

82
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:16,006
and here and there,

83
00:03:16,006 --> 00:03:19,119
a thin column of steam coming straight out of the ground.

84
00:03:19,572 --> 00:03:21,314
It is the most beautiful

85
00:03:21,314 --> 00:03:23,127
place you have ever seen.

86
00:03:23,127 --> 00:03:24,219
It is also,

87
00:03:24,219 --> 00:03:25,731
you will soon discover,

88
00:03:25,731 --> 00:03:29,164
the most beautiful place you have ever been cold.

89
00:03:29,164 --> 00:03:30,470
The settlers had a

90
00:03:30,470 --> 00:03:32,839
tradition about how to find a home.

91
00:03:32,839 --> 00:03:35,699
Iceland's Book of Settlements says that Ingólf,

92
00:03:35,699 --> 00:03:36,785
the first settler,

93
00:03:36,785 --> 00:03:39,446
threw his high-seat pillars overboard,

94
00:03:39,446 --> 00:03:41,081
the carved posts from his hall,

95
00:03:41,081 --> 00:03:43,520
and settled where the sea washed them ashore.

96
00:03:43,520 --> 00:03:45,211
His slaves found them at the

97
00:03:45,211 --> 00:03:47,202
place that became Reykjavík.

98
00:03:47,688 --> 00:03:49,372
Other settlers did the same,

99
00:03:49,372 --> 00:03:50,765
according to the sagas.

100
00:03:50,765 --> 00:03:52,064
You consider throwing

101
00:03:52,064 --> 00:03:53,473
your pillars overboard.

102
00:03:53,473 --> 00:03:56,126
Then you remember that you paid good

103
00:03:56,126 --> 00:03:58,249
money for them, and that the sea has

104
00:03:58,249 --> 00:04:00,926
never once agreed with you about anything.

105
00:04:00,926 --> 00:04:03,601
The same book also tells of an early

106
00:04:03,601 --> 00:04:04,749
explorer named Flóki,

107
00:04:04,749 --> 00:04:07,599
who sailed with three ravens to help him find land.

108
00:04:07,599 --> 00:04:09,766
He released them one by one,

109
00:04:09,766 --> 00:04:12,916
and the third flew on ahead, towards the coast.

110
00:04:12,916 --> 00:04:14,908
The ravens make a better

111
00:04:14,908 --> 00:04:16,471
story than what happened next.

112
00:04:16,471 --> 00:04:18,097
According to the book,

113
00:04:18,097 --> 00:04:21,560
Flóki's crew spent the summer fishing and forgot to make hay.

114
00:04:21,967 --> 00:04:24,643
That winter, all their livestock died.

115
00:04:25,098 --> 00:04:25,865
In spring,

116
00:04:25,865 --> 00:04:29,602
Flóki climbed a mountain, saw a fjord full of sea ice,

117
00:04:29,602 --> 00:04:32,199
and named the whole country Iceland.

118
00:04:32,556 --> 00:04:33,660
The name stuck.

119
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:34,970
So did the lesson.

120
00:04:34,970 --> 00:04:36,625
When the crew got home,

121
00:04:36,625 --> 00:04:40,118
the book says, they gave three very different reviews.

122
00:04:40,118 --> 00:04:43,007
Flóki said the land was worthless.

123
00:04:43,445 --> 00:04:45,191
A man named Herjólf said

124
00:04:45,191 --> 00:04:47,274
it had good points and bad.

125
00:04:47,274 --> 00:04:49,538
And a man named Thorolf claimed

126
00:04:49,538 --> 00:04:52,568
that butter dripped from every straw.

127
00:04:52,568 --> 00:04:53,400
From then on,

128
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:55,526
he was known as Butter-Thorolf.

129
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:57,064
You can guess which

130
00:04:57,064 --> 00:04:58,610
review reached your valley.

131
00:04:58,610 --> 00:05:00,688
Iceland's own medieval historians tell

132
00:05:00,688 --> 00:05:03,207
the story of how the island was settled.

133
00:05:03,207 --> 00:05:04,737
They date Ingólf's arrival,

134
00:05:04,737 --> 00:05:06,485
the first permanent settlement,

135
00:05:06,485 --> 00:05:08,690
to around the year eight hundred and seventy-four.

136
00:05:08,690 --> 00:05:11,516
Even he was not quite the first.

137
00:05:11,516 --> 00:05:13,298
The Book of Settlements says that

138
00:05:13,298 --> 00:05:15,057
before the Norse came,

139
00:05:15,057 --> 00:05:18,621
Irish Christian hermits, called papar, had lived there,

140
00:05:18,621 --> 00:05:21,869
and left behind Irish books, bells and croziers.

141
00:05:21,869 --> 00:05:22,862
Ari the Wise,

142
00:05:22,862 --> 00:05:27,358
Iceland's first historian, adds that they left because they

143
00:05:27,358 --> 00:05:30,049
did not want to live alongside heathens.

144
00:05:30,503 --> 00:05:31,949
Archaeologists have never found

145
00:05:31,949 --> 00:05:33,786
clear proof that they were there.

146
00:05:34,112 --> 00:05:35,943
But even Iceland's founding story

147
00:05:35,943 --> 00:05:38,389
begins with somebody who arrived earlier.

148
00:05:38,389 --> 00:05:41,102
Archaeology agrees surprisingly well.

149
00:05:41,572 --> 00:05:42,899
In central Reykjavík,

150
00:05:42,899 --> 00:05:45,520
excavators found a scrap of turf wall built

151
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:48,392
shortly before a layer of volcanic ash fell,

152
00:05:48,392 --> 00:05:49,857
in about eight hundred and seventy-one,

153
00:05:49,857 --> 00:05:51,918
give or take two years.

154
00:05:51,918 --> 00:05:53,682
It is one of the oldest human

155
00:05:53,682 --> 00:05:56,217
structures ever found in Iceland.

156
00:05:56,217 --> 00:05:58,973
After that, the settlers came fast.

157
00:05:58,973 --> 00:06:00,403
Medieval Icelandic writers

158
00:06:00,403 --> 00:06:02,097
said that the whole country was

159
00:06:02,097 --> 00:06:04,380
settled within about sixty years.

160
00:06:04,380 --> 00:06:05,620
Archaeologists still

161
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:07,791
argue about the exact pace.

162
00:06:07,791 --> 00:06:09,768
Nobody argues about the result.

163
00:06:09,768 --> 00:06:11,261
By the time you arrive,

164
00:06:11,261 --> 00:06:12,616
the best land is gone.

165
00:06:12,616 --> 00:06:14,947
There had been rules for claiming it,

166
00:06:14,947 --> 00:06:16,447
at least in later memory.

167
00:06:16,447 --> 00:06:18,006
One version of the Book of

168
00:06:18,006 --> 00:06:20,686
Settlements says that a man could claim no

169
00:06:20,686 --> 00:06:22,726
more land than he and his crew

170
00:06:22,726 --> 00:06:25,577
could carry fire around in a single day.

171
00:06:25,577 --> 00:06:27,765
A woman, the same version says,

172
00:06:27,765 --> 00:06:30,791
could claim as much as she could walk around

173
00:06:30,791 --> 00:06:33,250
on a spring day, from sunrise to sunset,

174
00:06:33,250 --> 00:06:36,000
leading a well-fed two-year-old heifer.

175
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,320
The historian Jesse Byock points

176
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,214
out that nobody knows for certain

177
00:06:40,214 --> 00:06:42,794
whether a king ever set such rules.

178
00:06:42,794 --> 00:06:45,084
But a saga does show a settler named

179
00:06:45,084 --> 00:06:48,028
Thorolf carrying fire around his land claim,

180
00:06:48,028 --> 00:06:50,555
marking his new boundary with flames.

181
00:06:50,555 --> 00:06:53,263
You find this extremely romantic.

182
00:06:53,263 --> 00:06:54,991
You also notice that nobody

183
00:06:54,991 --> 00:06:57,298
is handing out fire anymore.

184
00:06:57,298 --> 00:06:58,189
By your time,

185
00:06:58,189 --> 00:07:01,206
the land has owners, and owners have prices.

186
00:07:01,206 --> 00:07:03,327
Written sources describe land

187
00:07:03,327 --> 00:07:04,645
that was claimed,

188
00:07:04,645 --> 00:07:08,521
given, seized and, more and more often, bought.

189
00:07:08,521 --> 00:07:09,481
So you buy.

190
00:07:09,481 --> 00:07:12,510
The farm you can afford is a narrow

191
00:07:12,510 --> 00:07:14,649
valley in the north, the land a settler's

192
00:07:14,649 --> 00:07:17,047
grandson has decided he does not need.

193
00:07:17,047 --> 00:07:18,577
The meadow is small,

194
00:07:18,577 --> 00:07:22,523
the hillside is stony, and the river is mostly rocks.

195
00:07:22,523 --> 00:07:24,440
You pay with most of the silver

196
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:25,573
from your old farm,

197
00:07:25,573 --> 00:07:28,689
two of your cows, and a good deal of pride.

198
00:07:28,689 --> 00:07:30,430
The seller assures you

199
00:07:30,430 --> 00:07:32,139
the fishing is excellent.

200
00:07:32,139 --> 00:07:34,958
The seller is moving to the coast.

201
00:07:34,958 --> 00:07:37,147
You are not the only latecomer.

202
00:07:37,147 --> 00:07:38,768
A few years after you,

203
00:07:38,768 --> 00:07:41,113
according to the Book of Settlements,

204
00:07:41,113 --> 00:07:43,575
a man named Thorvald and his son,

205
00:07:43,575 --> 00:07:48,228
a red-haired youth called Eirik, leave Norway because of some

206
00:07:48,228 --> 00:07:51,626
killings and take land on the remote north-western coast,

207
00:07:51,626 --> 00:07:53,624
at a farm called Drangar.

208
00:07:53,624 --> 00:07:56,151
A university project on Norse

209
00:07:56,151 --> 00:07:58,626
history describes it as rather poor land.

210
00:07:58,626 --> 00:08:00,003
Remember that young man.

211
00:08:00,003 --> 00:08:02,342
The first thing you need is a house,

212
00:08:02,796 --> 00:08:04,399
and the first thing you notice

213
00:08:04,399 --> 00:08:06,509
is that there are almost no trees.

214
00:08:06,946 --> 00:08:08,715
Not real trees, anyway.

215
00:08:08,715 --> 00:08:11,913
Just low birch scrub, bent by the wind.

216
00:08:11,913 --> 00:08:14,121
It was not always like this.

217
00:08:14,798 --> 00:08:16,561
When the first settlers arrived,

218
00:08:16,561 --> 00:08:19,811
birch woodland covered a large part of the island.

219
00:08:19,811 --> 00:08:21,962
The Icelandic Forest Service estimates

220
00:08:21,962 --> 00:08:25,029
between a quarter and two fifths of the land.

221
00:08:25,029 --> 00:08:25,819
Today,

222
00:08:26,192 --> 00:08:29,241
birch woodland covers about one and a half percent.

223
00:08:29,241 --> 00:08:31,975
The settlers cleared it for pasture,

224
00:08:31,975 --> 00:08:33,992
for building and for fuel,

225
00:08:34,429 --> 00:08:37,743
and their grazing animals kept it from growing back.

226
00:08:37,743 --> 00:08:38,799
By your time,

227
00:08:38,799 --> 00:08:42,616
three generations in, the damage is well under way.

228
00:08:42,616 --> 00:08:45,502
Your neighbors help you raise the frame,

229
00:08:45,502 --> 00:08:47,751
and you promise to help with theirs.

230
00:08:47,751 --> 00:08:48,481
In Iceland,

231
00:08:48,481 --> 00:08:51,571
you discover, nobody builds alone,

232
00:08:51,571 --> 00:08:56,166
and everybody remembers exactly who owes whom a day's work.

233
00:08:56,166 --> 00:08:57,931
So you build with what

234
00:08:57,931 --> 00:09:00,227
Iceland has plenty of: grass.

235
00:09:00,729 --> 00:09:02,492
Your house is made of turf,

236
00:09:02,492 --> 00:09:04,755
cut into blocks and stacked

237
00:09:04,755 --> 00:09:06,688
into walls around a frame of wood.

238
00:09:06,688 --> 00:09:07,958
The finished walls can

239
00:09:07,958 --> 00:09:09,523
be about two metres thick.

240
00:09:09,523 --> 00:09:12,017
They are warm, they keep out the wind,

241
00:09:12,017 --> 00:09:14,145
and they make your home feel like

242
00:09:14,145 --> 00:09:16,065
living inside a very comfortable hill.

243
00:09:16,678 --> 00:09:18,844
The wooden frame is the hard part.

244
00:09:18,844 --> 00:09:20,881
Long, straight timber is scarce.

245
00:09:20,881 --> 00:09:22,860
You can import it from Norway at

246
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:24,240
a terrible price,

247
00:09:24,597 --> 00:09:27,512
or walk the beaches looking for driftwood that the

248
00:09:27,512 --> 00:09:30,054
ocean has carried in from far away.

249
00:09:30,054 --> 00:09:32,194
The historian Jesse Byock notes that

250
00:09:32,194 --> 00:09:34,476
driftwood beams were often short and

251
00:09:34,476 --> 00:09:37,896
imperfect once the rotten parts were cut away.

252
00:09:37,896 --> 00:09:39,184
Yours certainly are.

253
00:09:39,184 --> 00:09:40,775
One rafter is so twisted

254
00:09:40,775 --> 00:09:42,696
that your sons call it the Snake.

255
00:09:42,696 --> 00:09:44,541
It holds up the roof anyway.

256
00:09:44,541 --> 00:09:46,348
Inside, the light comes mostly

257
00:09:46,348 --> 00:09:49,030
through the smoke holes above the hearth.

258
00:09:49,030 --> 00:09:51,994
Turf houses did not last forever either.

259
00:09:51,994 --> 00:09:52,901
In Iceland,

260
00:09:52,901 --> 00:09:56,512
a turf longhouse lasted perhaps fifty to a hundred

261
00:09:56,512 --> 00:09:58,936
years before it had to be rebuilt.

262
00:09:58,936 --> 00:10:01,479
You are building for your grandchildren,

263
00:10:01,479 --> 00:10:03,673
whether they like twisted rafters or not.

264
00:10:04,223 --> 00:10:05,507
In your first autumn,

265
00:10:05,507 --> 00:10:07,609
an old man from the next farm comes

266
00:10:07,609 --> 00:10:09,571
by to inspect your haystacks.

267
00:10:09,571 --> 00:10:11,602
He finds them inadequate.

268
00:10:11,602 --> 00:10:12,743
Then he tells you about

269
00:10:12,743 --> 00:10:14,810
the summer the sun turned red.

270
00:10:14,810 --> 00:10:16,022
He was a boy then.

271
00:10:16,022 --> 00:10:19,030
The ground split open far to the south,

272
00:10:19,030 --> 00:10:21,301
and fire poured out of it for months.

273
00:10:21,301 --> 00:10:23,837
Ash fell, the sun went dim and bloody,

274
00:10:23,837 --> 00:10:25,422
and the summer was cold.

275
00:10:26,020 --> 00:10:28,306
He says many animals died that winter.

276
00:10:28,306 --> 00:10:30,796
Modern science has found his eruption.

277
00:10:30,796 --> 00:10:32,309
It is called Eldgjá.

278
00:10:32,309 --> 00:10:35,329
Researchers led by the University of Cambridge,

279
00:10:35,329 --> 00:10:37,646
using ice cores from Greenland,

280
00:10:37,646 --> 00:10:40,235
date it from around the spring of

281
00:10:40,235 --> 00:10:41,125
nine hundred and thirty-nine to at

282
00:10:41,125 --> 00:10:43,564
least the autumn of nine hundred and forty.

283
00:10:43,564 --> 00:10:45,992
It poured out around twenty

284
00:10:45,992 --> 00:10:47,762
cubic kilometres of lava,

285
00:10:47,762 --> 00:10:50,928
the largest lava flood in Iceland's history.

286
00:10:50,928 --> 00:10:52,663
Chronicles in Ireland,

287
00:10:52,663 --> 00:10:55,761
Germany and Italy describe a blood-red,

288
00:10:55,761 --> 00:10:59,090
weakened sun, and tree rings show one of the coolest

289
00:10:59,090 --> 00:11:01,848
summers of the last fifteen hundred years.

290
00:11:01,848 --> 00:11:03,996
He may well be right.

291
00:11:03,996 --> 00:11:06,004
The researchers think the young colony

292
00:11:06,004 --> 00:11:07,886
probably suffered badly,

293
00:11:07,886 --> 00:11:11,659
though no Icelandic text describes the eruption directly.

294
00:11:11,659 --> 00:11:15,351
They also suggest that the famous poem Völuspá,

295
00:11:15,351 --> 00:11:17,008
with its fire and its end of

296
00:11:17,008 --> 00:11:20,123
the old gods, may carry memories of it.

297
00:11:20,123 --> 00:11:23,002
The old man has never heard of Völuspá.

298
00:11:23,002 --> 00:11:25,871
He just knows to keep extra hay.

299
00:11:25,871 --> 00:11:28,816
You remember Flóki and his dead livestock.

300
00:11:29,223 --> 00:11:30,040
That winter,

301
00:11:30,040 --> 00:11:32,910
you count your haystacks every week.

302
00:11:32,910 --> 00:11:34,040
Then every day.

303
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:36,743
Your sons start hiding them from you,

304
00:11:36,743 --> 00:11:38,162
just to keep you calm.

305
00:11:38,162 --> 00:11:40,618
The whole point of a farm in Iceland,

306
00:11:40,618 --> 00:11:41,892
as Byock puts it,

307
00:11:41,892 --> 00:11:45,609
is to provide the fodder needed to keep a minimum

308
00:11:45,609 --> 00:11:47,535
of livestock alive through the winter.

309
00:11:47,535 --> 00:11:48,674
Grass is wealth.

310
00:11:49,255 --> 00:11:50,644
Hay is survival.

311
00:11:50,644 --> 00:11:52,904
Your cows are not pets.

312
00:11:53,277 --> 00:11:56,093
They are a very slow, very hungry bank.

313
00:11:56,093 --> 00:11:58,827
Your first winter is long and dark.

314
00:11:58,827 --> 00:12:00,410
The cows lose weight.

315
00:12:00,912 --> 00:12:01,593
So do you.

316
00:12:01,593 --> 00:12:05,292
Halla starts speaking to you again around midwinter,

317
00:12:05,292 --> 00:12:06,134
mostly to tell

318
00:12:06,134 --> 00:12:08,212
you which animal looks the thinnest.

319
00:12:08,212 --> 00:12:09,748
Her loom never stops.

320
00:12:10,266 --> 00:12:13,571
Wool is the one thing Iceland has in abundance,

321
00:12:13,571 --> 00:12:15,595
and in a few generations,

322
00:12:15,595 --> 00:12:17,295
as Aslaug could have told you,

323
00:12:17,295 --> 00:12:20,692
Icelandic law will turn woven cloth into money.

324
00:12:20,692 --> 00:12:22,298
Halla is quietly the

325
00:12:22,298 --> 00:12:24,021
richest person in the house.

326
00:12:24,021 --> 00:12:25,153
She knows it.

327
00:12:25,511 --> 00:12:26,819
You suspect it.

328
00:12:26,819 --> 00:12:28,209
But you survive.

329
00:12:28,209 --> 00:12:31,218
In spring, the meadow turns green,

330
00:12:31,218 --> 00:12:33,962
the lambs arrive, and you discover that your

331
00:12:33,962 --> 00:12:36,166
new neighbor across the river believes that

332
00:12:36,166 --> 00:12:38,526
the boundary between your farms follows a

333
00:12:38,526 --> 00:12:41,148
stream that you are quite sure has moved.

334
00:12:41,148 --> 00:12:43,043
Some things travel well.

335
00:12:43,674 --> 00:12:46,570
Fortunately, Iceland has an answer to this,

336
00:12:46,570 --> 00:12:48,410
and it is the reason you

337
00:12:48,410 --> 00:12:49,972
will spend two weeks every summer

338
00:12:49,972 --> 00:12:53,220
sleeping in a booth on a lava field.

339
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:54,658
In nine hundred and thirty,

340
00:12:54,658 --> 00:12:57,019
the Icelanders founded the Althing,

341
00:12:57,019 --> 00:13:00,723
a national assembly that met every year at Thingvellir,

342
00:13:00,723 --> 00:13:02,039
in the south-west.

343
00:13:02,525 --> 00:13:04,869
It met for about two weeks in summer.

344
00:13:04,869 --> 00:13:07,338
Free men came from all over the island,

345
00:13:07,338 --> 00:13:08,883
and camped in booths,

346
00:13:08,883 --> 00:13:12,539
temporary structures of stone and turf roofed with cloth.

347
00:13:12,977 --> 00:13:13,892
It was court,

348
00:13:13,892 --> 00:13:17,131
parliament, market and festival all at once.

349
00:13:17,472 --> 00:13:19,234
Iceland had no king.

350
00:13:19,234 --> 00:13:21,907
Instead, it had a lawspeaker,

351
00:13:21,907 --> 00:13:24,059
who stood on the Law Rock and recited

352
00:13:24,059 --> 00:13:26,843
the law aloud, a third of it each year,

353
00:13:26,843 --> 00:13:28,716
so that over his three-year term,

354
00:13:28,716 --> 00:13:30,788
everyone heard the whole thing.

355
00:13:31,258 --> 00:13:32,130
Before writing,

356
00:13:32,130 --> 00:13:34,010
the law lived in his memory.

357
00:13:34,544 --> 00:13:35,193
In time,

358
00:13:35,193 --> 00:13:39,482
the Althing would even decide the island's religion.

359
00:13:39,482 --> 00:13:40,992
Around the year one thousand,

360
00:13:40,992 --> 00:13:44,432
according to the Book of Settlements and the sagas,

361
00:13:44,432 --> 00:13:46,731
Iceland took Christianity into

362
00:13:46,731 --> 00:13:48,493
its law there, so that the whole

363
00:13:48,493 --> 00:13:51,754
country would have one law and one religion.

364
00:13:51,754 --> 00:13:54,753
Your first Althing is a revelation.

365
00:13:54,753 --> 00:13:56,428
The booth you share with three

366
00:13:56,428 --> 00:13:57,896
other farmers leaks,

367
00:13:57,896 --> 00:13:59,550
the speeches are long,

368
00:13:59,550 --> 00:14:03,218
and everybody seems to be related to everybody else.

369
00:14:03,218 --> 00:14:05,347
You have never been happier.

370
00:14:05,347 --> 00:14:06,939
Hundreds of people,

371
00:14:06,939 --> 00:14:09,873
and every one of them with a grievance.

372
00:14:09,873 --> 00:14:11,759
You feel at home at last.

373
00:14:11,759 --> 00:14:14,594
You bring your stream to the Althing.

374
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:16,574
So does your neighbor.

375
00:14:16,574 --> 00:14:18,576
Witnesses are called.

376
00:14:18,576 --> 00:14:20,865
Old men remember things differently.

377
00:14:20,865 --> 00:14:23,444
Eventually, the case is settled,

378
00:14:23,444 --> 00:14:25,724
mostly in your neighbor's favor.

379
00:14:26,210 --> 00:14:27,731
And here is the catch.

380
00:14:27,731 --> 00:14:29,978
The Althing could judge,

381
00:14:29,978 --> 00:14:32,827
but it had no power to carry out its judgments.

382
00:14:32,827 --> 00:14:34,346
The winning side had to

383
00:14:34,346 --> 00:14:36,080
enforce the verdict itself,

384
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:38,750
with the help of family and friends.

385
00:14:38,750 --> 00:14:42,578
The most serious punishment was full outlawry:

386
00:14:42,578 --> 00:14:43,684
a man lost his

387
00:14:43,684 --> 00:14:46,124
property and all protection,

388
00:14:46,515 --> 00:14:49,734
and nobody was allowed to feed or shelter him.

389
00:14:49,734 --> 00:14:52,788
For a man with friends, the system worked.

390
00:14:52,788 --> 00:14:55,871
For a man with few friends and a new farm,

391
00:14:55,871 --> 00:14:57,963
it was a lesson in politics.

392
00:14:58,466 --> 00:15:01,083
You go home and start making friends.

393
00:15:01,083 --> 00:15:03,690
It is much harder than making hay.

394
00:15:03,690 --> 00:15:05,017
When you visit the coast,

395
00:15:05,017 --> 00:15:06,696
you notice something else.

396
00:15:07,069 --> 00:15:09,468
Not everyone in Iceland came from Norway,

397
00:15:09,468 --> 00:15:11,665
and not everyone came willingly.

398
00:15:11,665 --> 00:15:14,226
DNA studies of modern and ancient

399
00:15:14,226 --> 00:15:16,196
Icelanders suggest that a large

400
00:15:16,196 --> 00:15:18,228
share of the first settlers' women had

401
00:15:18,228 --> 00:15:20,811
their roots in Scotland and Ireland.

402
00:15:20,811 --> 00:15:22,044
Many of them,

403
00:15:22,044 --> 00:15:25,289
researchers suggest, probably came as slaves.

404
00:15:25,289 --> 00:15:28,459
The Book of Settlements remembers Irish thralls,

405
00:15:28,459 --> 00:15:30,477
and even names islands after them,

406
00:15:30,477 --> 00:15:32,646
as we saw in our chapter on thralls.

407
00:15:32,646 --> 00:15:34,502
On the farm next to the harbour,

408
00:15:34,502 --> 00:15:36,665
an old woman sings to her grandchildren

409
00:15:36,665 --> 00:15:39,003
in a language you do not understand.

410
00:15:39,003 --> 00:15:41,186
Nobody has written down her name.

411
00:15:41,186 --> 00:15:41,427
Her

412
00:15:41,427 --> 00:15:44,353
great-grandchildren will be Icelanders.

413
00:15:44,353 --> 00:15:46,115
And somewhere back in Norway,

414
00:15:46,115 --> 00:15:48,889
in your old valley, a young man called

415
00:15:48,889 --> 00:15:51,909
Colm is still cutting turf for Hallvard.

416
00:15:51,909 --> 00:15:53,266
So why was being a Viking

417
00:15:53,266 --> 00:15:55,707
settler in Iceland worse than you think?

418
00:15:56,064 --> 00:15:57,252
Because the frontier

419
00:15:57,252 --> 00:15:58,780
closed before you arrived.

420
00:15:58,780 --> 00:16:00,141
The free land was gone,

421
00:16:00,141 --> 00:16:02,884
the trees were going, the hay was never enough,

422
00:16:02,884 --> 00:16:04,886
and the ground itself could catch fire.

423
00:16:04,886 --> 00:16:07,120
And because even at the edge of the world,

424
00:16:07,120 --> 00:16:08,606
you still had a neighbor,

425
00:16:08,606 --> 00:16:10,989
and a boundary, and an argument.

426
00:16:10,989 --> 00:16:13,289
And yet, you built something.

427
00:16:13,289 --> 00:16:16,252
A turf house under a twisted rafter,

428
00:16:16,252 --> 00:16:18,612
a herd that survived the winter,

429
00:16:18,612 --> 00:16:21,333
and a voice at an assembly with no king.

430
00:16:21,333 --> 00:16:25,114
For a quarrelsome farmer from a small Norwegian valley,

431
00:16:25,114 --> 00:16:26,567
that is not nothing.

432
00:16:27,182 --> 00:16:30,193
Thirty years later, you are an old man,

433
00:16:30,551 --> 00:16:33,221
and that red-haired youth from Drangar,

434
00:16:33,221 --> 00:16:35,048
now known as Eirik the Red,

435
00:16:35,048 --> 00:16:37,573
no relation to your old neighbor,

436
00:16:37,931 --> 00:16:41,038
is recruiting settlers for a new land in the west.

437
00:16:41,717 --> 00:16:43,228
He calls it Greenland.

438
00:16:43,228 --> 00:16:45,657
The Book of Settlements says he chose

439
00:16:45,657 --> 00:16:47,742
the name because people would be more eager

440
00:16:47,742 --> 00:16:50,112
to go there if the land had a good name.

441
00:16:50,112 --> 00:16:50,746
It worked.

442
00:16:50,746 --> 00:16:52,709
According to the same book,

443
00:16:52,709 --> 00:16:56,107
about twenty-five ships set out from Iceland,

444
00:16:56,107 --> 00:16:57,781
around nine hundred and eighty-five.

445
00:16:57,781 --> 00:16:58,740
Fourteen arrived.

446
00:16:58,740 --> 00:17:00,357
Some were driven back,

447
00:17:00,357 --> 00:17:02,731
and some were lost at sea.

448
00:17:02,731 --> 00:17:04,238
Your sons want to go.

449
00:17:04,238 --> 00:17:06,706
You tell them that the grass is never

450
00:17:06,706 --> 00:17:08,945
greener on the other side of the sea.

451
00:17:08,945 --> 00:17:10,740
They point out that you

452
00:17:10,740 --> 00:17:12,185
are the one who said it was.

453
00:17:12,185 --> 00:17:14,130
And somewhere behind the turf house,

454
00:17:14,130 --> 00:17:15,995
your sheep has escaped again,

455
00:17:15,995 --> 00:17:17,959
onto your neighbor's land.

456
00:17:17,959 --> 00:17:20,436
This is Viking Chapters.

457
00:17:20,436 --> 00:17:22,642
The lives behind the legends.

458
00:17:22,642 --> 00:17:24,305
If you would like to see another

459
00:17:24,305 --> 00:17:26,090
life behind the legends,

460
00:17:26,090 --> 00:17:29,096
subscribe, and we will meet in the next chapter.
